      "code": "DEV",
      "category_name": "Model Development",
      "status": "BLOCKED",
      "prerequisites": [
        "DEV_1"
      ],
      "details": "We addressed non-independence by employing hierarchical (multilevel) models to accommodate the non-independence effect. Additionally, to represent repeated measures through time, we accounted for a model with a heteroscedastic autoregressive (HAR) structure (Viechtbauer, 2010). This approach allows variances to differ across time periods when non-independence occurs."
    },
    "DEV_3": {
      "code": "DEV",
      "category_name": "Model Development",
      "status": "BLOCKED",
      "prerequisites": [
        "DEV_2"
      ],
      "details": "We used fixed- and random-effect specifications, interpreting global heterogeneity using Cochran\u2019s Q."
    },
    "DEV_4": {
      "code": "DEV",
      "category_name": "Model Development",
      "status": "BLOCKED",
      "prerequisites": [
        "DEV_3"
      ],
      "details": "We further extended the analyses by including various \u201cmoderators\u201d, describing catchment (e.g., climate, soil texture, permafrost) and fire attributes (e.g., severity, burn extent) in mixed-effect meta-regression. This approach allowed us to evaluate whether any of the moderators could explain the variability in DOC responses."
    },
    "EXEC_1": {
      "code": "EXEC",
      "category_name": "Execution & Compute",
      "status": "BLOCKED",
      "prerequisites": [
        "INFRA_1",
        "DEV_4"
      ],
      "details": "All modeling was executed in R using the metafor package (Viechtbauer, 2010), specifically employing the \u201crma.mv\u201d function to control for potential pseudo-replication."
    },
    "EVAL_1": {
      "code": "EVAL",
      "category_name": "Evaluation & Output",
      "status": "BLOCKED",
      "prerequisites": [
        "EXEC_1"
      ],
      "details": "A multi-level model showed a mean DOC increase of 26 % (95 % CI: 15\u201338 %), peaking in the second post-fire year (23 % (95 % CI: 3 % to 47 %), and attenuation thereafter. Responses varied with catchment and fire attributes."
